<BaseLayout title="Why propose another optical audio transport?" description="The design problem behind an open Layer 2 optical audio proposal, and what it does not claim.">
  <article class="narrow page-intro prose"><p class="eyebrow">Engineering note</p><h1>Why propose another optical audio transport?</h1><p class="lead">The proposal starts with a practical gap: domestic optical links are simple, but their common format envelope and one-way clock relationship are narrow.</p><p>TOSLINK solved a useful problem. A fibre connection can avoid a conductive path between the two audio ports, and a correctly implemented link can deliver its supported stream without errors. O2AT should not dismiss that installed base to make a case for change.</p><p>The question is whether a standard pluggable optical module and ordinary Ethernet framing can carry a broader, negotiated audio stream while keeping conversion timing under receiver control. The v0.2 answer is a proposal built around 1G SFP, a fixed header, capability exchange, receiver credits and an elastic FIFO.</p><p>That architecture has costs. Two endpoints must agree on framing, session state, loss handling and capability declarations. A bridged Ethernet domain requires admission control. An endpoint must still prove that its USB, FPGA, DAC and clock hardware can accept and deliver the requested mode.</p><p>The bandwidth tables are calculations, not listening tests. They account for Ethernet overhead and compare with MAC service. They do not establish latency bounds through an arbitrary switch, clock-noise performance or an audible benefit.</p><p>The next step is not a claim of superiority. It is a reproducible implementation that independent teams can build, inspect and measure against the published vectors.</p><p><a href="/specification/">Read the v0.2 source →</a></p></article>
</BaseLayout>
